.TH SYS_PROC_MAPS 2 "2026-05-09" "Substrate" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
sys_proc_maps \- list memory mappings for a process
.SH LIBRARY
System library (libsys, \-lsys)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/sysinfo.h>
.PP
.BI "int sys_proc_maps(pid_t " pid ", sys_map_t *" maps ", size_t *" count ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR sys_proc_maps ()
walks the
.B vm_areas
list of process
.I pid
and writes one
.B sys_map_t
record per region:
.PP
.RS
.nf
typedef struct sys_map {
    uint32_t start;     /* inclusive */
    uint32_t end;       /* exclusive */
    uint32_t flags;     /* prot in low byte, vm_flags in next byte */
    char     name[256]; /* file name, or "[anon]" */
} sys_map_t;
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.B flags
field packs both halves of the kernel's mapping metadata: the
protection bits (PROT_READ/WRITE/EXEC) live in the low byte, and
internal flags (VM_SHARED, VM_GROWSDOWN, ...) live in the next byte.
Userspace recovers them with
.BR "(flags & 0xFF)"
and
.BR "(flags >> 8) & 0xFF" .
.PP
On entry
.I *count
is the array capacity; on return it is the actual region count.
Probe with
.I maps
=
.B NULL ,
.I *count
= 0.
.PP
.I pid
= 0 means the calling process.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0 on success, a negated errno on failure.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ESRCH
.I pid
does not refer to an existing process.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I maps
or
.I count
points outside the caller's address space.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_proc_info (2),
.BR mmap (2)
